{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "number": 1,
  "title": "Prop 1 \u2014 Housing bonds: what would the money buy?",
  "fps": 30,
  "frames": 5406,
  "checked": "2026-10-19",
  "narration": "asset://334ef54be7acca3fac1224beb010fcaab75606d2e706f36510f09bc78d48290b.wav",
  "prompter_words": "words-global.json",
  "script": [
    {
      "name": "The big picture",
      "narration": "Proposition one asks whether California should borrow money for housing. A bond supplies money now, while repayments and interest stretch across future budgets. Some affordable housing already receives public grants or low-cost loans. Developers combine that assistance with other financing, usually in exchange for keeping homes affordable to eligible households. This measure authorizes eleven point two five billion dollars: ten billion for housing programs, and one point two five billion for veterans\u2019 home loans. Those two portions have different repayment sources."
    },
    {
      "name": "An illustrative example",
      "narration": "Imagine a renter looking for an affordable apartment. An apartment project might have land and a builder but still need cheaper financing to offer below-market rents. A public loan can help close that gap. This illustrates the mechanism, not a guarantee of a particular rent. A housing counselor explains the available programs."
    },
    {
      "name": "How it would work",
      "narration": "The largest allocation is seven point two billion dollars to acquire, build, or renovate multifamily rental housing. Projects generally reserve units for lower-income households for fifty-five years. Other allocations support homeownership, infrastructure, farmworker housing, student housing, and tribal housing. Eligibility depends on the particular program; passing the bond does not give every applicant a home. The analyst estimates subsidies for up to forty thousand multifamily units. That includes housing acquired or renovated, so it is not a promise of forty thousand additional newly built homes. The General Fund would repay the housing portion at about five hundred to six hundred million dollars a year for roughly twenty-five years. Veterans\u2019 loan payments would repay their separate bond portion."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote yes",
      "narration": "A voter might support financing affordable homes sooner rather than waiting for enough money in annual budgets. Supporting construction and preserving restricted rents can help specific households even when the broader market remains expensive."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote no",
      "narration": "You might ask whether the long commitment is worthwhile. A skeptical voter could prefer paying as funds become available, or addressing permitting and construction costs first. Interest adds expense, and future repayments compete with other services. Money alone does not remove every obstacle to building."
    },
    {
      "name": "What polling indicates",
      "narration": "In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 55 percent said yes, 43 percent no, and 2 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not votes already cast."
    },
    {
      "name": "Make your own decision",
      "narration": "Yes authorizes this borrowing; no rejects this bond, while existing housing programs continue. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links, and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice."
    }
  ],
